; The Chaosnet transmit list sends its packets oldest first (CHAOS-XMT-0
; and CHAOS-XMT-DONE, sys/ucadr/uc-chaos.lisp).  Load lisp/chaos-transmit-order.lisp
; first.  CTO-RUN pushes four full-length packets back to back while the
; first is on the cable and notes the order in which they leave the transmit
; list.  MIT's microcode sends and keeps the head and pops the head at
; Transmit Done: a push while a packet is on the cable makes it free the new
; packet unsent and send the old one again, so 0, 2, 1, 0 go on the cable and
; 3 never does, and they leave the list as (3 2 1 0).  Run with muir's
; --chaos-trace (through the --quux wrapper) to see the cable: the four go to
; hosts 177270 to 177273.
;
; The control: one packet alone leaves the list.
(progn (cto-run 1) (cto :order)) => (0)
; The precondition: all four are on the list after the last push, the first
; still on the cable.  If not, the check below means nothing.
(progn (cto-run) (cto :window)) => "window entered"
(cto :length) => 4
; The microseconds between the pushes, and the order.
(cto :gaps)
(cto :order) => (0 1 2 3)
; Again, to see it is not a matter of timing.
(progn (cto-run) (list (cto :window) (cto :order))) => ("window entered" (0 1 2 3))
